===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|French fries}}

===Etymology===
Clipping of earlier {{m|en|French fried potatoes}} (1856) and {{m|en|French-fried potatoes}}, potatoes supposedly prepared in the [[French]] style.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-PerfectSoundWhatever-french fries.wav|a=CA}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|p|sg=french fry}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|North America|Philippines}} Strips of [[deep-fried]] [[potatoes]] that have been [[french|frenched]] (cut into strips). {{defdate|from 1903}}
#: {{ux|en|'''French fries''' are our specialty.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Lillian Pettengill|title=Toilers of the Home|year=1903|publisher=Doubleday, Page & Company|location=New York|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/toilershomereco00pettgoog/page/n310/mode/1up|page=292|passage=“What's the matter?” she asked quickly. “Haven't we had all the things before? Soup, chops, peas, '''French fries''', and the fruit pudding–there wasn't a thing new.”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|chapter=XXII|publisher=Henry Holt & Company|title=Love Conquers All|page=111|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/loveconquersall00bencgoog/page/n141/mode/1up|author=[[w:Robert Benchley|Robert C. Benchley]]|passage=His first official act was to order dinner. “A nice, juicy steak,” he is said to have called for, “'''French fries''', apple pie and a cup of coffee.” It is probable that he really said “a coff of cuppee,” however, as he was a wag of the first water and loved a joke as well as the next king.}}

====Usage notes====
In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, deep-fried potato strips are generally and traditionally called '''''[[chip]]s'''''. However, with exposure to the "french fries" on the menus of US-origin fast food outlets, the term "french fries" (or just "fries") has for many people come to mean, specifically, "chips" of the type usually served in those outlets, which are generally thinner, and also possibly crispier, than the traditional British "chip".

